-- title:
--   Root states before and after a descendant block switches
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values. Fix the switching experiment at terminal level $N$ and a node $v$ of level $k\le N$.
--
--   1. **Before block.** $\rho(\mathrm{hi}(v))$, the root state in which the descendant block of $v$ still carries its labels (all leaves from $\mathrm{hi}(v)$ on carry $b$).
--   2. **After block.** $\rho(\mathrm{lo}(v))$, the root state just after the whole block has switched to $b$.
--
--   The difference of the two states is the sum of the leaf increments in the block:
--   $$
--   \rho(\mathrm{hi}(v))-\rho(\mathrm{lo}(v))=\sum_{\mathrm{lo}(v)\le s<\mathrm{hi}(v)}\pi(s)\,u_s .
--   $$
